WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:05.770
SAMRAT SAHA: Hello, everybody.

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:07.170
I'm Samrat Saha.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.210
I am a customer engineering
manager for Google Cloud,

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:11.900
and I'm based out of Wisconsin--

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:13.333
Milwaukee, Wisconsin.

00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:15.250
Thank you for taking the
time to join me today

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.860
to understand our
perspective on how

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.670
to deliver a flawless UX for
Black Friday to Cyber Monday.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.820
So on our journey
today, we will-- we're

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:27.240
going to cover a few things.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:28.782
For instance, we're
going to go over,

00:00:28.782 --> 00:00:30.660
what is Black
Friday/Cyber Monday?

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.350
What does success mean?

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.400
What is UX/CX?

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.220
How do you prepare for it?

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.200
How you execute on it?

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.220
And a wrap-up.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.470
And we're going to go over
quite a few concepts today,

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:46.780
and the goal is to give
an idea as to how complex

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.660
an undertaking can be,
and how partnerships

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:53.380
can help you solve for a
business critical time period.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.000
So we'll start off with
design for failure.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.480
All things fail.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.810
No matter how strong you
make it, it is going to fail.

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.680
It's how you recover that
makes all the difference.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.290
And this is something that
people find odd coming

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:14.612
from an organization that's
known for reliable services,

00:01:14.612 --> 00:01:17.070
but that's precisely sort of
the mind shift or the paradigm

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:19.320
shift that you need
to get used to.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:20.430
Things fail.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:24.600
And how you recover define
how successful you become.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.472
So just to put
things in context,

00:01:26.472 --> 00:01:27.930
Black Friday to
Cyber Monday-- it's

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.380
one of my favorite holidays.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:30.750
I love Thanksgiving.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.560
For retailers, it's a really
important time period.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.130
For instance, in
2019, Black Friday

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.620
saw sales that was over $7
billion in digital sales.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:43.470
It is up 14% from 2018.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:47.340
And in 2019, Cyber Monday
had greater than $9 billion

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:48.090
in sales.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:51.250
That was a 19% jump
from the previous year.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.340
So to put things in
perspective, that's

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.970
a substantial amount of sales
that's happening over a very

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:58.880
compressed time frame.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.610
You're literally going from
Thanksgiving to the Monday

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:08.280
after, and you see that much
sales come through time period.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.160
So BFCM-- you know, Black
Friday to Cyber Monday--

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.580
execution impact--
when it goes well,

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:16.860
obviously your sales go up.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:17.730
Revenue goes up.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.205
It's great for business.

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:21.060
What happens if
it goes sideways?

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.570
What happens, let's say, if
you have an outage for now?

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.160
What is the impact on the brand?

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.620
What is the impact on the
customer relationship?

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.050
What does a 1% impact mean?

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.120
These are really important
business questions

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.430
that need to be answered, and
this is where cloud can help.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:40.320
UX and CX-- user experience,
customer experience--

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:41.340
what is it?

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:46.440
Well, a lot of people
now have mobile phones.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:51.820
And a lot of people are exposed
to the latest and greatest

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:54.560
when it comes to how
applications work,

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:55.950
how websites work.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.610
That's the user experience.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:00.540
So for instance, if you're
shopping for something,

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.080
and you decide to go and add
three items to your shopping

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.490
cart, was that a
smooth experience?

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:08.400
Did the brand make
you jump through hoops

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.710
to do that action?

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.380
How smooth was the
overall transaction?

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:16.980
A good user experience
enables your users

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.470
to achieve the tasks that
they want to achieve.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.850
And customer experience is
sort of the overall umbrella

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:22.350
around it.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:23.580
It's not just the app.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.480
It's how you communicate
with your customer.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:27.610
It is how you can
get help if needed.

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:30.030
So that's the overall sort
of relationship, if you will.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.430
UX is very sort of specific.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:36.720
And invariably,
having a great UX

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.120
can make the difference between
making a sale versus not

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.040
making a sale.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.720
So for instance, what
does a one second delay

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.480
mean to your website?

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.870
A 1% delay can mean around a
7% reduction in conversions.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:56.050
It can mean a poorer user
experience for your users,

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:59.970
and that could also mean
a loss in business, a move

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.050
to a competitor.

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:04.130
So UX and CX are pretty
critical in terms

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:05.880
of maintaining the
relationship and making

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.990
sure your users actually
enjoy using your products.

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:11.910
And after all, it is
all about the user.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:18.110
So UX/CX during Black
Friday to Cyber Monday

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:20.290
the stakes go much higher.

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:23.370
So for instance, if you're
delivering a great user

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:26.520
experience when it's not
Black Friday to Cyber Monday,

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:27.520
that's great.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:30.060
But can you give a similar
experience when there's

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.810
much higher load on the system?

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:36.210
So for instance, let's say that
a person can go in and buy,

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:37.920
let's say, a pair of shoes.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.710
And that entire thing can be
done with minimal disruption,

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.270
and the user loves it.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:44.280
Now, during Black
Friday to Cyber Monday,

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.530
let's say there are
thousands of users

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:48.510
trying to do the same
thing at the same time.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:50.490
Can you deliver
the same experience

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:54.090
that made your customer love
your brand during a high stakes

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:54.810
period?

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.185
So that becomes
really important.

00:04:56.185 --> 00:04:57.003
Stakes go up.

00:04:57.003 --> 00:04:58.670
The revenue potential
is so much higher,

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:00.090
but so is the business impact.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:03.510
If things go down, how
do you recover from that?

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:05.130
What happens when,
let's say, that you

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:10.740
have users in the east coast and
you have users in the midwest?

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:13.080
Can you give similar
user experiences

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:14.975
to both sets of users?

00:05:14.975 --> 00:05:16.350
Because it wouldn't
be right-- it

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:18.780
wouldn't be a consistent
experience with your brand

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:22.370
if somebody, say, in Milwaukee
has a three times higher load

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:24.845
time than somebody,
say, in New York City.

00:05:24.845 --> 00:05:27.220
So these are considerations
that become really important,

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:30.113
but again, it amplifies so
much more during Black Friday

00:05:30.113 --> 00:05:30.780
to Cyber Monday.

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:35.610
To help you understand
this, to help you figure out

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:38.757
what is important versus
what is not important,

00:05:38.757 --> 00:05:41.090
I would encourage you to think
in terms of critical user

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:42.240
journeys.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.800
So what is important
to your user?

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.970
What is important
to your business?

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:48.440
So for instance, a user--

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:51.540
it might be really important
to buy, say, crayons

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.817
so they can ship it to
their niece in Florida.

00:05:53.817 --> 00:05:55.150
Now that can be super important.

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:56.733
That user journey
is really important.

00:05:56.733 --> 00:05:58.770
Now, for your
business, it might mean

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:02.790
that you want to have a
feedback survey filled out,

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:04.410
because you get information.

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:05.970
You can better your service.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:08.470
But to a user, that may
not be as important.

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:10.608
But there are zones
of intersection

00:06:10.608 --> 00:06:12.150
between what's
important to your user

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:13.920
and what's important
to your business.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:15.753
And when you figure out
where it intersects,

00:06:15.753 --> 00:06:17.495
it can help guide
you in terms of where

00:06:17.495 --> 00:06:18.870
do you need to
put more resources

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:20.170
to harden those systems.

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:21.630
So for instance,
checkout is super

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:23.520
important to your
business and your user,

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:26.640
because a user wants to go
buy stuff, have it shipped,

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:28.620
and complete it with
minimal disruption.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.690
For a business, it is
extremely important, too,

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.970
because that's a straight
line to the money.

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:36.180
So you can figure out what
the intersection points are

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:38.770
to help guide you design your
systems, harden your systems,

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.910
and provide the best user
experience for a business

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:43.110
critical time period.

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:44.580
So why is it so hard?

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:46.650
I kind of alluded
to this previously.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:48.150
During Black Friday
to Cyber Monday,

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:50.850
you could see up to 3x to
4x, three times or four times

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:52.830
the traffic than
you would normally.

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:54.650
And there is no--

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:56.540
it may not be a steady pattern.

00:06:56.540 --> 00:06:58.010
It could be very spiky traffic.

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:01.220
So you might see a whole bunch
of users hit your systems

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:03.482
within the first hour of
your sale starting up,

00:07:03.482 --> 00:07:05.690
or it could be something
like you have several spikes

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:09.170
because you have promotions
going out every other hour.

00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:14.060
So the traffic is very spikey,
and it is substantially larger

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.685
than what you'd normally
find outside of Black Friday

00:07:16.685 --> 00:07:18.710
to cyber Monday.

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:22.760
So with that in mind, how
do you account for that?

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:24.330
Traditionally, what
you would do is

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:25.830
you would have a
set of compromises.

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:27.110
You would say, you know what?

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:29.570
Black Friday to Cyber
Monday is critical to me.

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:31.070
In order to meet
that demand, I'm

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:35.340
going to over provision my
resources throughout the year.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:36.920
So now you're paying
for redundancy

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:38.110
for the rest of the year.

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:39.818
Or you compromise and
say, you know what?

00:07:39.818 --> 00:07:41.210
I'm willing to take the hit.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:43.550
I'm going to sort of
under provision knowing

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:47.390
that it has a slightly negative
impact on my customers,

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:48.390
but that's a compromise.

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:52.600
And that's why having your
resources in the cloud,

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:56.120
having cloud native sort of
mentality towards developing

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:58.730
your solutions can really help.

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:00.350
Are your resources elastic?

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:03.030
Can they grow and
shrink with the usage?

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:07.280
Are they redundant
across different regions?

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:10.208
What happens when, say,
a region goes down?

00:08:10.208 --> 00:08:11.750
These are the kind
of things that you

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:13.640
have to bring to the
table when you're

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:17.060
trying to address a very
distributed user base who

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:19.980
have high expectations
on a user experience,

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.530
because that's
kind of what you've

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:23.150
grown to build with your users.

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:25.220
You've built a
relationship that you

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:27.590
want to protect and nurture.

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:31.700
And with all that comes in stuff
like you have a distributed

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:33.860
system.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:36.770
How does a database
handle asset transactions?

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:38.730
How do you guarantee
that consistency?

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:40.897
These are the sort of things
that start cropping up,

00:08:40.897 --> 00:08:43.220
which are not normally seen
in traditional monolithic

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:48.210
application, not normally seen
in traditional deployments.

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:50.240
So these are things that
make it much harder,

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:52.880
and it usually takes a
completely different approach

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:54.510
to be successful in the cloud.

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:55.940
But the payoffs are there.

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:57.020
The payoff, as well.

00:08:57.020 --> 00:08:59.260
The payoff is, again, a
great user experience.

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:01.130
I cannot stress this enough.

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:03.830
What the goal should be
is provide the best user

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:07.610
experience so that a user
can enjoy working the brand

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:10.500
and building the
relationship up with you.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:14.030
So what does it look
like kind of internally?

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:17.510
And this is fairly sort
of standard in terms

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:19.220
of how people approach--

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:22.320
how teams approach
approaching peak.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:23.320
You have capacity plans.

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:26.210
You've got to figure out how
much capacity you'll need.

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:27.530
You have load testing.

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:29.720
You have tabletop exercisers.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:32.830
So this is stuff like you
sit down and figure out, OK,

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:34.730
our database has gone down.

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:36.620
What do you do then?

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:39.740
Let's say you decide you're
going to simulate a network

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:41.647
outage to the east coast.

00:09:41.647 --> 00:09:42.480
What do you do then?

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:43.910
How do you feel?

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:46.862
Then obviously you have
Black Friday to Cyber Monday,

00:09:46.862 --> 00:09:48.320
and sometimes it's
code-- depending

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:50.300
the organization
you are, sometimes

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:51.470
you institute a code freeze.

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:54.080
Sometimes you don't.

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:57.470
It depends on your approach
to how you develop products.

00:09:57.470 --> 00:09:59.270
Black Friday to Cyber Monday--

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:01.760
and then tapering
towards December

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:03.410
you start scaling down.

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:05.830
So you understand your capacity.

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:06.860
You scale up.

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:10.520
You test, deliver,
and scale down.

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:13.490
So all this is a very,
very compressed timeline

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:16.760
of very sort of--
a lot of activities

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:19.470
going on just to deliver
on that short time frame.

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:22.100
So breaking down, first of
all, definition of success.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:23.900
What does success mean?

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:26.510
This is a business
critical objective.

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:29.630
What does it mean for a business
in order to be successful?

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:31.640
Once you have that, you
can start figuring out,

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:34.670
what do you need to
do to deliver on that?

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:36.890
Then you have core
activities-- load testing.

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:39.740
Do you test to three
times to four times

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:42.540
the amount of load
that you normally get?

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:44.720
Tabletop exercises, which
I just went through,

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:48.140
do you assimilate sort of
different failure scenarios?

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:50.790
And you figure out, what are
the lines of communication?

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:53.105
How do you respond to outages?

00:10:53.105 --> 00:10:54.980
Disaster recovery
testing-- this is something

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:57.290
that Google is famous for.

00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:58.920
This is where you
figure out things--

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:00.148
again, things are going down.

00:11:00.148 --> 00:11:00.940
How do you recover?

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:02.065
How quickly do you recover?

00:11:02.065 --> 00:11:04.030
Where do you recover to?

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:07.040
Failover-- let's say
that you have regions--

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:10.360
compute regions
in east and west.

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:13.370
Sake of argument, let's
say east goes down.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:16.790
How do you failover so that
your users on the east coast

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:18.920
are not significantly impacted?

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:20.480
There is a degradation
of service.

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:21.440
That's for sure.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:24.440
The latency went up, but how do
you make sure that they still

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:25.550
can use your service?

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:26.770
And that's key.

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:28.170
Rollback strategies.

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:30.830
What happens when you decide
to push an update out?

00:11:30.830 --> 00:11:32.220
Can you roll back smoothly?

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:35.150
Let's say that the update
caused a bug that was not caught

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:36.770
until it was in production.

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:38.530
Sometimes these
things do happen.

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:43.580
Can you actually roll
back and with confidence?

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:46.710
Communication-- how do
you communicate failures?

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:48.470
How do you communicate outages?

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:50.990
What is that internal
business process?

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:52.640
And most importantly,
who decides?

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:53.867
Who's the decision maker?

00:11:53.867 --> 00:11:55.450
So let's say that
we decide that we're

00:11:55.450 --> 00:11:59.020
going to failover
from central to west

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:00.790
for the sake of argument.

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:02.123
Who's going to pull that button?

00:12:02.123 --> 00:12:03.665
Who's gonna push
that button, rather?

00:12:03.665 --> 00:12:04.960
Who makes that final decision?

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:07.350
Are those chains
of communication--

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:09.490
have they been
clearly demarcated?

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:11.980
And are teams empowered
to make those business

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:13.780
critical decisions?

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:18.040
So again, fairly complex,
but for really good reasons.

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:19.655
Now, do you have
the architecture

00:12:19.655 --> 00:12:22.330
to deliver at peak?

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:24.298
Is your application--
or your application--

00:12:24.298 --> 00:12:25.090
are they monoliths?

00:12:25.090 --> 00:12:27.830
Was it distributed
services, micro services?

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:30.620
What kind of cloud design
patterns are you using?

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:31.960
Are these multi region systems?

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:33.400
Are these distributed systems?

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:36.490
High availability
disaster recovery?

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:38.555
4-9's, 5-9's reliability.

00:12:38.555 --> 00:12:40.680
You've got to figure out
what your error budget is.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:42.730
You've got to figure
out how much effort

00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:46.060
are you willing to put in to get
a certain kind of reliability.

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:48.070
Because nothing is going
to be 100% reliable.

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:50.860
You've got to figure
out your trade off.

00:12:50.860 --> 00:12:52.390
Implication of
your systems layer.

00:12:52.390 --> 00:12:54.110
The implication of
your compute layer.

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:55.540
The implication of scaling.

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:59.680
So again, the architecture that
can help you deliver for peak

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:01.670
becomes really,
really important.

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:06.700
So this is a sample
architecture of, let's say,

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:09.130
an e-commerce-ish application.

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:10.803
And there's certain
things that are

00:13:10.803 --> 00:13:12.595
pretty common in this
kind of architecture,

00:13:12.595 --> 00:13:15.550
but the key is you
start with the user.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:20.320
You have users distributed
across the nation or globally,

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:26.060
and the fundamental part is
you route a user, if you will,

00:13:26.060 --> 00:13:28.330
to the resources
that is required

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:31.570
to complete their user journey
that's closest to be used.

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:33.437
In this case, what
you're seeing is

00:13:33.437 --> 00:13:34.770
you have region one on the left.

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:36.730
You have region
two on the right.

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:39.100
Connecting those two is a
cloud load balancer on top.

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:41.633
So think of it as a request
coming in from the user.

00:13:41.633 --> 00:13:43.300
Hitting the load
balance, which decides,

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:47.800
should I send the user's
request to the east,

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:50.740
or should I send the
user request to the west?

00:13:50.740 --> 00:13:52.928
Region one or region two.

00:13:52.928 --> 00:13:54.970
Within those regions, you
have compute resources.

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:57.190
In this case, you have GKE.

00:13:57.190 --> 00:14:01.540
You have-- essentially that's
more for Microsoft's kind

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:02.410
of approach.

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:04.270
Then you have a
persistence layer.

00:14:04.270 --> 00:14:07.330
You have Cloud C could have
NCache or Datastore, whatever

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:09.490
is important to your business.

00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:11.770
Underlying all that, you
have an analytics layer.

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:13.340
So BigQuery.

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:17.410
And the architecture is a
distributed architecture.

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:19.240
It's a highly
available architecture.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:23.470
If a region goes down,
and you resume services,

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:26.200
in this architecture you can.

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:27.370
Is this repeatable?

00:14:27.370 --> 00:14:30.010
What if you decide to
stand up a third region?

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:31.570
This architecture
is modular enough

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:34.930
that you can repeat what was
successful in both regions

00:14:34.930 --> 00:14:38.330
and stand up another region.

00:14:38.330 --> 00:14:42.470
This allows you to also
test pushing out updates.

00:14:42.470 --> 00:14:45.280
So again, it's a very
distributed architecture,

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:48.160
and it gives you the
flexibility to scale up.

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:53.410
It gives you the safety to
withstand regional outages.

00:14:53.410 --> 00:14:56.200
It also gives you the
template, if you will,

00:14:56.200 --> 00:14:58.540
to expand out as
your business grows.

00:14:58.540 --> 00:14:59.800
Now, this is a fairly--

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:01.540
these are some of
the services that you

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:04.242
would have in a traditional
sort of e-commerce application.

00:15:04.242 --> 00:15:06.700
As you can see, you have a
whole bunch of digital services.

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:10.090
You have search,
taxonomy, a whole bunch

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:15.010
of services that are required
for a user to be successful.

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:17.550
And you have other services
that are non-digital,

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:19.400
like shopping, delivering,
stuff like that.

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:21.970
But again, what I'm trying
to impress on you is,

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:24.940
to deliver a great
e-commerce application,

00:15:24.940 --> 00:15:26.410
there are a whole
bunch of services

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:28.700
that need to be built
out, and there's

00:15:28.700 --> 00:15:30.870
a lot of cloud
native design patters

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:34.870
that you've got to
implement to be successful,

00:15:34.870 --> 00:15:39.500
especially because, again, are
your services elastic enough?

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:42.790
Is your infrastructure elastic
enough, resilient enough

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:47.320
to handle high loads, and to
allow for disaster recovery

00:15:47.320 --> 00:15:50.270
to happen if things go
badly, and also scaled down

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:52.030
once you've executed on peak?

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:54.360
So this is a topic
all by itself.

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:55.880
I did want to mention it--

00:15:55.880 --> 00:15:58.030
site reliability engineering.

00:15:58.030 --> 00:16:00.590
This is very user impact driven.

00:16:00.590 --> 00:16:07.150
This is more about for an
infrastructure for somebody

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:08.890
who maintains say
hardware, alright,

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:12.520
so let's say for an
example, or somebody who's

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:15.280
developing an application.

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:16.740
Things of interest
might be, what

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:20.650
is the CPU percentage, of
utilization percentage?

00:16:20.650 --> 00:16:22.900
Now those are interesting
things and are important

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:27.850
pieces of information that
help you solve for problems.

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:30.460
But what is the
impact on the user?

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:32.740
If you don't start approaching
it from user impact--

00:16:32.740 --> 00:16:34.810
for instance, let's
say that you want

00:16:34.810 --> 00:16:39.940
a user to be able to browse for
the products on your website,

00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:43.560
and you want the search results
to be returned within, say,

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:45.280
one second.

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:49.210
Now, if that is impacted, now
that's a user level impact,

00:16:49.210 --> 00:16:51.430
and that can help guide the
business better in terms

00:16:51.430 --> 00:16:56.180
of solving for a better system
that is providing such results.

00:16:56.180 --> 00:17:02.190
Now the underlying cause might
be, let's say, CPU utilization.

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:05.150
But if you're not guided
by the impact on users,

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:08.599
it becomes very hard to figure
out what is important for users

00:17:08.599 --> 00:17:10.710
and what is important
for business.

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:16.190
I would encourage you to go into
SRE and get a lot of resources

00:17:16.190 --> 00:17:19.310
on-- at Google Cloud to help
you understand, where does SRE

00:17:19.310 --> 00:17:20.210
fit in?

00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:23.900
And how do you develop and
deliver reliable systems

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:26.690
at velocity so that your
developers, your product teams

00:17:26.690 --> 00:17:30.140
can make cool stuff,
but they can also

00:17:30.140 --> 00:17:34.750
deliver stuff reliably, and
maintain reliability, as well.

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:36.160
Common threads of success--

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:38.690
so in my time at Google,
I've seen some teams

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:42.120
be very successful in
delivering a great, great Black

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:43.550
Friday to Cyber Monday.

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:46.520
And some of the things that
I've noticed that my teams as

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:48.450
well have noticed
that helps people

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:52.800
be successful-- there are clear
objectives, communication.

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:53.670
Great communication.

00:17:53.670 --> 00:17:59.210
People know-- your objectives
are being communicated clearly.

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:02.360
The teams are empowered
to make decisions.

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:07.080
And this trust-- they embrace
cloud native thinking.

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:08.490
Again, it's designed for scale.

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:11.300
The elasticity that is important
to deliver on those experiences

00:18:11.300 --> 00:18:12.320
is really important.

00:18:12.320 --> 00:18:14.210
And then the power
of partnerships.

00:18:14.210 --> 00:18:16.070
Leverage your partners.

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:19.460
They have helped solve some
of these problems for others.

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:21.800
And more importantly,
it is in their interest

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:23.430
to help you be successful.

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:27.920
So it's sort of the
all-embracing approach that is

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:28.670
defined--

00:18:28.670 --> 00:18:32.780
that is sort of guided
by overall objectives--

00:18:32.780 --> 00:18:34.520
business objectives,
user objectives.

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:36.230
Success on GCP--
as you can see, we

00:18:36.230 --> 00:18:39.740
have quite a few
of our customers

00:18:39.740 --> 00:18:44.720
who have partners with-- partner
with GCP to be successful.

00:18:44.720 --> 00:18:49.520
And hoping that we
can help out you

00:18:49.520 --> 00:18:51.430
achieve your business goals.

00:18:54.350 --> 00:18:55.980
Thank you.

